From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:25:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio: keep the primary input's frames to real time

The primary input takes the frames its client asks for from a pipe that
an I/O thread fills with the buffers the device captures. When the pipe
was short it made silence up for the missing frames (a warning in the
log each time), and its sleep pacing made some up to keep a schedule of
its own. A recording thus held a gap of silence about that often, the
captured frames behind it came as late as the gap was long, and the
timestamps of the input could not be smooth.

The device paces the input, so the pacing of its own goes, with its
members. A read waits for its frames, and the I/O thread wakes the wait
each time it has written to the pipe; the wait ends when the thread is
stopped.

The count has to follow the time as well, because a recorder stamps what
it reads from the time of the read. When the device delivers nothing for
a while (the worker of the sound device blocked, a busy host), a read
that gave up after its bounded wait and made up only its own frames
left the count behind the time by the stall: a recording held less
audio than its timestamps spanned, a player that follows the audio
clock skipped ahead at every gap, and clips played back too fast.

So the frames of the client are due one after the other from a clock
that starts with the first transfer after the stream starts, pauses,
goes to standby or changes its device. A read waits for its frames
until 100 ms after the last of them is due, which covers what the
device delivers late in the normal course, and makes the missing ones
up then: the count follows the time through a stall of the device, and
nothing is made up while the device delivers. A stall leaves the count
behind the clock by that slack and the delay of the reader, so a stall
of any length is made up while the reader comes back within the slack.
A reader that is later than that during a stall restarts the clock
once, and the stall leaves a gap of its own.

Elapsed time between two reads restarts nothing by itself. A count that
is more than 200 ms behind the clock as a read starts or ends does:
the whole process stood still, and the video of the recording has the
same gap, so the standstill is not caught up and stays a gap in the
timeline. A read that it stopped holds one buffer of silence for it at
most. The limit is the slack plus the delay of the reader that a stall
leaves room for, and bounds how long before a reply a frame was due.

The count may lead the clock by the slack at most: frames that would
put it further ahead are dropped, oldest first. They are the buffers
delivered before the clock started, those for time that was made up
already after a stall, and what a device that is faster than the clock
delivers in excess. The drop is logged at most every ten seconds, with
the frames dropped since.

The device's clock and the guest's differ a little. A read that got all
its frames from the device moves the start of the clock by a small step
(at most 1 ms) towards a lateness of 10 ms, so that a long recording
neither reaches the edge of the slack nor falls out of it, and a count
that a reader's absence left behind the clock (the ring of the device
overran and lost frames) is back at that lateness within seconds.
